---
name: PR Description Writer
description: >
  Paste your git diff and get a structured pull request description that
  explains the why, not just the what. Includes a test plan, risk flags, and
  reviewer callouts. Makes your PRs easier to review and faster to ship.
installCommand: git diff main...HEAD | pbcopy
---

# PR Description Writer

Paste your `git diff` and get a professional PR description that actually helps reviewers understand the change. Covers the motivation, what changed, how to test it, and what to watch for.

## Prompt

```
You are a senior engineer writing a pull request description for a code review. Your goal is to make the reviewer's job as easy as possible.

Git diff / changes summary:
[PASTE YOUR GIT DIFF OR DESCRIBE YOUR CHANGES HERE]

Ticket/issue context (optional):
[LINK OR DESCRIPTION OF THE ISSUE BEING SOLVED]

Write a PR description using this exact format:

---

## Summary

[1–3 sentence executive summary: what this PR does and why it exists. Write this for someone who won't read the rest of the description.]

## Motivation

[Why was this change needed? Link to the bug report, user complaint, or business requirement. If it's a refactor, explain what pain it eliminates.]

## What changed

[Use bullet points. Each bullet should be one logical change. Be specific — name the files, functions, or components affected. Do NOT just list file names; explain what changed about them.]

- **[Component/File]:** [what changed and why]
- **[Component/File]:** [what changed and why]

## How to test

[Step-by-step test plan. Be specific enough that a reviewer unfamiliar with this feature can verify it works.]

1. [Setup step if needed]
2. [Do X]
3. [Verify Y appears / Z happens]
4. [Edge case to check]

**Automated tests:** [List new tests added, or explain why no new tests were needed]

## Screenshots / recordings

[Note if UI changed: "see screenshots below" or "no visual changes". Leave this section if there are no visual changes.]

## Risk and rollback

**Risk level:** [Low / Medium / High] — [one sentence explanation]

**Rollback plan:** [How to undo this if it causes issues in production]

**Feature flag:** [Yes/No — if yes, what flag and how to disable]

## Reviewer notes

[Anything specific you want reviewers to look at closely, design decisions you made that they might question, or things you deliberately did NOT change and why.]

---

After the PR description, add a separate section:

## Review checklist (for the author)

Go through the diff and flag:
- [ ] Any hardcoded values that should be config
- [ ] Any missing error handling
- [ ] Any new dependencies added (and why they were chosen over alternatives)
- [ ] Any backward-incompatible changes (API, database schema, config format)
- [ ] Performance implications (N+1 queries, unnecessary re-renders, blocking calls)
- [ ] Security considerations (user input, authentication, authorization)
```

## Git commands to generate the diff

```bash
# Full diff against main branch
git diff main...HEAD

# Summary of files changed (good for large PRs)
git diff --stat main...HEAD

# Just the commit messages (for context)
git log --oneline main...HEAD

# Combine for full context
echo "=== Commits ===" && git log --oneline main...HEAD && echo "" && echo "=== Diff ===" && git diff main...HEAD
```

## Tips

- For large PRs (>500 lines), paste only the `--stat` summary and describe the changes — the prompt works with summaries too
- The "Reviewer notes" section is the most valuable part — use it to pre-answer the questions reviewers will ask
- If you have a Jira/Linear ticket number, always link it — it gives reviewers the business context that makes code decisions obvious
- Run the review checklist yourself before requesting review; it catches ~60% of common review comments before they're made

## Variations

### Minimal mode (for small PRs)

```
Write a concise PR description for this change. Include: what changed (2–3 bullets), why, and how to test it (2–3 steps). Keep it under 200 words.

Diff: [paste diff]
```

### Security review focus

```
Write a PR description for this change with a security-focused review section. Highlight: what user input is processed, what auth checks exist, what data is stored or transmitted, and what attack vectors this change opens or closes.

Diff: [paste diff]
```
